		Shopkeeper: Of course. And I'll answer each and every one of them, as best I can. => tutorial

~ tutorial
Shopkeeper: First things first. I'll need to explain how the Cullings really work. 
Shopkeeper: Want the long version or the short one?
- Long version. 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=laugh]I'm starting to like you!
	Shopkeeper: Besides infecting people with spots of corruption that quite literally suck the life out of them, the Cullings also yield a residual substance that attaches itself to worldly, man-made items. 
	Shopkeeper: While the manner in which said corruption targets and attaches itself to people or items is seemingly random, there's surely a logic and a purpose to it, known only to the Goddess Herself.
	Shopkeeper: Whenever the sky turns red in the morning - a harbinger of the Cullings, as you well know - some people die, and some items become corrupted. 
	Shopkeeper: While the goodwill of our Goddess has fortunately kept us safe from the former, we have not been unaffected by the latter. 
	Shopkeeper: Interesting thing is, the residual substance that attaches itself to objects is invisible to the human eye. 
	Shopkeeper: Anyone can see the spots of corruption that cause people to die gruesome deaths. But only a few of us, who have had the good fortune of being blessed by the Goddess with Her Gift of sight, can see the spots of corruption that infect our everyday objects. 
	Shopkeeper: Though most people can't see said spots, their side effects can be felt by anyone. Using, interacting with or looking at an infected object can cause madness, ilness, and violent urges.
	Shopkeeper: Sometimes, people can even hear the object talking to them, inciting them to violence. No wonder they go mad. 
	Shopkeeper: Luckily, you and I can rid the items of their evil. And that's why people send them to us. 
	Shopkeeper: All we gotta do is, quite literally, rip the evil spots off like a bandaid using our feeble hands and fingers. => gameplay_tutorial
- Short version, please. 
	Shopkeeper: Perfectly understandable. Life is short.
	Shopkeeper: The Cullings are the work of the Goddess. As you well know, they happen once every few weeks or months when the sky turns red. 
	Shopkeeper: Some people die gruesome deaths, and some items become infected. 
	Shopkeeper: The infection on the items is usually invisible to the human eye. That's where we come in.
	Shopkeeper: As Gift-bearers, we're able to see the spots of corruption and cleanse the items of their evil. 
	Shopkeeper: All we gotta do is, quite literally, rip the evil spots off like a bandaid using our feeble hands and fingers. => gameplay_tutorial

~ gameplay_tutorial
#tutorial start
Shopkeeper: [do lock_player_action("complete_object", true)][do lock_player_action("complete_sticker", true)][do lock_player_action("focus_object", true)]There. Look down. There's an infected object on the table. Pick it up and put it down in front of you, for now. [do do_scripted_event("start_find_workbench_tutorial")] [do DialogueFuncs.add_object_to_workbench("tutorial_peel_book")] [do wait_for("first_drag_on_object")] [do do_scripted_event("stop_find_workbench_tutorial")]
#tutorial: look down, move objects
Shopkeeper: [do! do_scripted_event("start_focus_object_tutorial")]Now pick it up again and give it a proper look. Rotate it around, try to really focus on it. Make sure to put it back down after a few seconds. [do lock_player_action("focus_object", false)] [do wait_for("first_rotate_on_object")] 
#tutorial: focus mode, rotate 
Shopkeeper: Feels weird, eh? Focus on it long enough and you might just die. No, really, I'm not kidding.
Shopkeeper: You need constant breaks in our line of work. Whenever it starts to hurt, or when you feel like you might be losing it, put the item down. Take a moment to breathe.
Shopkeeper: Some of the side effects will stay with you till you get a good night's sleep. As the day progresses, you'll become more weary and your body's ability to deal with the side-effects will go down. You'll see.
Shopkeeper: Generally speaking, though, you'll be mostly fine after a short break. And then you're back to peeling.
Shopkeeper: Speaking of, let's try peeling the evil off this one. 
Shopkeeper: Pick it back up, focus on it, and run your fingers over those weird looking spots. Then peel. Remember, take breaks if you feel the need. [do lock_player_action("complete_sticker", false)] [do wait_for("first_sticker_cleansed_on_object")] 
#tutorial: managing your health, peeling 
Shopkeeper: Well done. Rid it of all corruption spots and then put it in the basket, to your right. [do lock_player_action("complete_object", false)] [do wait_for("object_completed")] => normal_spots_complete_check
#tutorial: done zone

#tutorial: Repeating normal spots object based on player performance 
~ normal_spots_complete_check
if Variables.get_var("prog_score_special_object_tutorial_peel_book") < 100
	% Shopkeeper: No worries, no one's an expert from the get go. Try again.
	% Shopkeeper: Almost. You've got it this time. 
	
	% Shopkeeper: You have to rid it of all corruption spots and then place it in the basket, to your right. [do DialogueFuncs.add_object_to_workbench("tutorial_peel_book")] [do wait_for("object_completed")] => normal_spots_complete_check
	% Shopkeeper: Fully cleanse the object and then put it in the basket, on the right hand side. [do DialogueFuncs.add_object_to_workbench("tutorial_peel_book")] [do wait_for("object_completed")] => normal_spots_complete_check
else 
	Shopkeeper: Well done! => spots_tutorial_end

~ spots_tutorial_end
Shopkeeper: That's it. Our day consists of doing this over and over again, taking short breaks whenever we feel the need, and longer ones once every two or three objects. Safer that way. Easy to get lost in this kind of work. 
Shopkeeper: Like I said, make sure you don't get too absorbed in it. Look around, talk to me or to whoever finds themselves in this sorry place, peer out the window, anything. Balance is key. 
Shopkeeper: You can keep practicing, if you so wish. These items are from old batches, anyway. Their owners are long dead. Mostly.
-  I want to practise a little more. 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=laugh]You're enjoying this, eh? Good. 
	Shopkeeper: I've got a few more items around the place. Here, you can have them. [do DialogueFuncs.add_object_to_workbench("flask")] [do DialogueFuncs.add_object_to_workbench("milk_carton_variant_02")]
	Shopkeeper: Just make sure to finish them up before you go to bed. Wouldn't want them just laying around here. => wow
- Think I got it. Let's move on.
	Shopkeeper: Alright then. I'll go check up on Sarah real quick, if you don't mind. She should've been up by now. Be right back.[do DialogueFuncs.play_sfx("sfx_door_close")] => END 

~ wow
if DialogueFuncs.get_object_count() == 0
	Shopkeeper: Oh, wow, you're quick. Looks like I'm already the second best cleanser in town. Well done.
	Shopkeeper: I'll go check up on Sarah real quick, if you don't mind. She should've been up by now. Be right back.[do DialogueFuncs.play_sfx("sfx_door_close")] => END 
else
	Shopkeeper: I'll go check up on Sarah while you work, if you don't mind. She should've been up by now. Be right back.[do DialogueFuncs.play_sfx("sfx_door_close")] => END
=> END